\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S1}{\ignorespaces The free-form residual statistic's one free choice, the regularization strength $\lambda $. (a) Completeness at $c=60$ on the full 1\,000-lens populations for the fitted macro-model at three $\lambda $ ($10^{5}$ is the value used throughout; 68\% Wilson intervals) and, on the 300-lens seed-0 subsample, for the exact macro-model at $\lambda =10^{5}$ (black stars, the ceiling at that $\lambda $; a fitted curve at another $\lambda $ can exceed it). $\lambda =10^{4}$, $10^{5}$ and $10^{6}$ in light to dark shades of one hue. At $\lambda =10^{4}$ the statistic is dominated by fitted noise and its low-mass points scatter about the 10\% chance line; a point below the line is a fluctuation. (b) False-positive rate on the two multipole populations at the same three $\lambda $ (hatched: $a_m/\theta _{\rm  E}=0.01$).}}{16}{}\protected@file@percent }
